package pages

import "saui/templates/layout"

templ Why(csrfToken string) {
	@layout.Base("Why SAUI", csrfToken, "/why", "/static/css/why.css") {
		<section class="page-hero">
			<div class="page-hero__content">
				<h1>Why</h1>
				<p class="lead">{ `The SPA era solved real problems. The mistake was applying that architecture universally.` }</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<article class="why-page">
			<section>
				<h2>What SPAs got right</h2>
				<p>{ `In 2013, Gmail and Google Maps genuinely required client-side state. A mail client
					that reloaded the page on every action was unusable. A mapping tool that lost your
					position on every interaction was broken. The SPA pattern solved a real problem
					for a specific class of application: one where the interaction model is fundamentally
					stateful and the latency of a server round-trip is the dominant UX constraint.` }</p>
				<p>{ `That class of application still exists and the SPA pattern remains correct for it.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>The universalisation mistake</h2>
				<p>{ `The error was treating the SPA architecture as the correct default for web development,
					regardless of whether the application shared any of the requirements that justified
					it. Food ordering apps. Banking dashboards. Healthcare booking systems. CMS tools.
					Internal admin panels.` }</p>
				<p>{ `None of these applications require the interaction model of Gmail. All of them were
					built as if they did.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section id="compounding-cost">
				<h2>The compounding cost</h2>
				<p>{ `When client-side state is the default, a synchronisation problem is introduced that
					did not previously exist: keeping the client copy of state consistent with the
					server copy. Solving that problem has generated entire engineering disciplines.` }</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>State management libraries.</strong>
						{ ` Redux, Zustand, Jotai, MobX, Recoil. Each exists to manage complexity
						that originates from the client holding state.` }
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Cache invalidation.</strong>
						{ ` React Query, SWR, Apollo. Libraries dedicated to the problem of knowing
						when the client data is stale.` }
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Optimistic updates.</strong>
						{ ` Showing the user a predicted outcome before the server confirms it.
						When the server disagrees, reconciliation is required.` }
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Hydration.</strong>
						{ ` The server renders HTML, the client re-renders the same HTML in JavaScript,
						then merges the two. A problem that only exists because the client needs to
						take ownership of the DOM.` }
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Build toolchains.</strong>
						{ ` Webpack, Vite, esbuild, Turbopack. Compilers, bundlers, and tree-shakers
						for a language that runs natively in the browser.` }
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>{ `These are not solutions to product problems. They are solutions to the synchronisation
					problem. They would not exist if the client did not hold state.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>The security consequence</h2>
				<p>{ `When state lives on the client, the client can tamper with it. The classic
					"isAdmin: true" attack is not a developer error. It is a structural consequence
					of the architecture. If the client holds a representation of the user permissions,
					the client can modify that representation.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Server-side validation is often added as a second layer, but the client-side
					representation persists as an attack surface. Under SAUI, there is no client-side
					state to tamper with because there is no client-side state.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>The security record</h2>
				<p>{ `The theoretical risk has a documented record. Between 2024 and 2026, the major
					frontend frameworks accumulated a consistent pattern of critical vulnerabilities,
					each traceable to the same architectural decisions that introduced client state
					and the infrastructure required to manage it.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Next.js middleware is the standard pattern for protecting routes: authentication,
					authorisation, and CSP headers all run there. In March 2025, a single HTTP header
					(x-middleware-subrequest) bypassed it entirely. CVE-2025-29927, rated 9.1 critical,
					gave unauthenticated access to every route the middleware was supposed to protect.
					The vulnerability existed because the Edge Runtime separates security logic from
					route handlers by design. The pattern the framework promoted was the attack surface.` }</p>
				<p>{ `React Server Components introduced a custom binary serialisation protocol for
					streaming server-rendered output. That protocol, parsed server-side without
					sufficient input validation, produced CVE-2025-55182: unauthenticated remote code
					execution via a single malformed HTTP request. Maximum CVSS score of 10.0.
					State-linked threat actors exploited it within hours of disclosure. LexisNexis
					failed to patch for three months and lost 3.9 million records, 53 plaintext
					cloud credentials, and federal personnel data.` }</p>
				<p>{ `The hydration boundary is a persistent XSS vector across every framework that
					uses it. Server state must be serialised into inline HTML so the client can
					reconstruct it. Data crossing that boundary without aggressive escaping produces
					exploitable XSS. React Query (CVE-2024-24558), React Router (CVE-2025-43865),
					Angular (CVE-2026-32635), and Nuxt (CVE-2024-34343) all demonstrated this.
					Angular's automatic template sanitisation, widely considered the strongest of
					any major framework, was bypassed when its i18n and security subsystems interacted.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Supply chain attacks completed the picture. The Shai-Hulud self-replicating npm
					worm, active from September 2025 through 2026, compromised packages across all
					three major framework ecosystems: React, Angular (ngx-bootstrap, ngx-toastr),
					and Vue (AntV). The Axios package, 70 million weekly downloads, was compromised
					by a North Korean state actor for three hours in March 2026. Polyfill.io, a CDN
					loaded by 384,000 sites, was sold to a threat actor who served malicious JavaScript
					conditionally to avoid detection. The average framework application carries
					1,000 to 2,000 transitive npm dependencies. Each is a potential vector.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Under SAUI, these attack classes do not apply. There is no hydration boundary
					because there is no client state to hydrate. There is no middleware security gate
					because authorisation runs inside the request handler, not in a separate runtime.
					There is no custom serialisation protocol because the server renders HTML directly.
					There are no npm dependencies because htmx ships as a single vendored file and
					the remainder of the stack is Go standard library. These are not mitigations.
					They are the result of not having the architectural layers that produced the vulnerabilities.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>What this is not</h2>
				<p>{ `This is not an argument that SPAs are bad, that React is wrong, or that JavaScript
					should be avoided. It is an argument about scope. The SPA pattern is appropriate
					for applications that share the requirements that justified it. Most applications
					do not.` }</p>
				<p>{ `SAUI is the web's original model (server renders truth, browser displays it)
					re-expressed with modern tooling that removes the historical limitations that made
					SPAs appealing. Slow servers are fast now. Partial page updates are available
					without a JS framework. Progressive enhancement is achievable without a build step.` }</p>
				<p><a href="/architecture">See how the architecture works</a></p>
			</section>
		</article>
	}
}
